course: dictionary
lesson: 4880
title: Idioms: when words mean more than they say
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An idiom is a group of words whose meaning you cannot get by adding up the separate words. «It's raining cats and dogs» does not mean animals are falling from the sky. It means it is raining very hard.
explain: Learn an idiom as one whole piece, like a key that opens one door. When you meet «spill the beans», don't picture beans on the floor: it means tell a secret. If the literal meaning makes no sense, you have probably found an idiom.
explain: Every language has idioms, and they sound odd when translated word for word. A Ukrainian gran says «вішати локшину на вуха», which sounds like hanging noodles on someone's ears, but it means telling someone nonsense. To translate an idiom, find the matching idiom, not the matching words.
fact: An idiom is a group of words with a meaning you cannot guess from the separate words.
fact: «Break a leg» means good luck, and «a piece of cake» means very easy.
fact: «Spill the beans» means tell a secret, and «hit the sack» means go to bed.
fact: «Under the weather» means ill, and «flat out like a lizard drinking» (Australia) means very busy.
fact: Idioms make writing lively, but they can confuse learners of English because they are not meant literally.
exercise: «It's raining cats and dogs.» What does this idiom mean? | The dogs are wet | *It is raining very hard | It is cold and windy | Animals are falling from the sky
exercise: «Don't spill the beans about the party!» What does spill the beans mean? | *Tell a secret | Cook a big meal | Drop food on the floor | Count the beans
exercise: Dad says, «Break a leg at the concert!» What does he mean? | *Good luck | Go to bed early | Be careful not to fall | Stop singing
test: Which idiom means go to bed? | Under the weather | Spill the beans | A piece of cake | *Hit the sack
test: «She was under the weather, so she stayed home.» What does under the weather mean? | Very busy | Very happy | Lost in the rain | *Ill
exam: Why do idioms confuse learners of English? | Because they have too many syllables | Because they are always written in capital letters | Because they are always about animals | *Because they are not meant literally
